    reportLabel: "THE DOT PLOT RESOLVED HAWKISH AND IT RESOLVED THE OPPOSITE WAY TO THIS FILE'S WARNING. For two days this file argued the June dots were a DOVISH landmine because futures priced more tightening than the committee had pencilled in. That was wrong, and the numbers say so: the 2026 median went to 4.1% from 3.8%, 16 of 18 participants want at least one more hike in the final two meetings of the year, and the projections revised growth UP to 2.3%, unemployment DOWN to 4.1% from 4.3% and core PCE UP to 3.4%. Warsh: this summer's inflation readings do not tell me that underlying trends have meaningfully improved. USD goes from +2 to +3 - the written trigger fired. Every dollar pair confirmed it overnight: DXY 100.265 with the week +1.23% and the month +0.61%, EUR/USD 1.14669, GBP/USD 1.33812, USD/JPY through 155 to 156.005, USD/CHF 0.82552. Second board change: CAD IS CUT FROM +1 TO 0 because its terms-of-trade leg broke, not because it was threatened - WTI's WEEKLY turned negative at -0.30% from +8.95%, Brent is -1.74% on the week, and Canadian services PMI collapsed to 46.8 from 49.1. That flips EUR/CAD and GBP/CAD out of SHORT and CAD/CHF out of LONG. Third, a genuine euro tailwind nobody was looking for: EU gas -5.20% and UK gas -6.08% ON THE WEEK, the first sustained fall in a month. Metals took the hawkish hit and refused to break - gold fell to about 4264 then bounced to 4298.04, +0.80%. Board: USD +3; EUR, AUD, JPY +1; GBP, CHF, CAD 0; NZD -1. BoE 11:00Z today, BoJ 02:30Z tomorrow.",
