  geopolitics: "The honest starting point today is that this file over-corrected yesterday and the correction needs correcting. On Tuesday this page had been repeating that Strait of Hormuz traffic was running roughly 90% below pre-war levels; yesterday it withdrew that figure on the strength of US Energy Secretary Chris Wright putting crude transits above 17 million barrels per day against roughly 20 million before the February 28 attacks, and Treasury Secretary Bessent citing at least 10 million a day. That withdrawal was too confident, because the two numbers are not measuring the same thing and only one of them is independently verifiable. IMF PortWatch recorded 8 vessel transits on September 13 against a pre-crisis baseline of 85 per day, independent trackers now put the disruption past its 190th day, and Al Jazeera has reported traffic falling even as Washington insists the waterway is fully open. Vessel counts down roughly 90% and official claims of crude volumes down 15-25% can both be published, but they cannot both be describing the same waterway in the same way, and the official figure is contested rather than confirmed. The practical instruction is to stop treating either number as settled and to watch what is not in dispute. What is not in dispute is where the squeeze actually binds. Refined products remain the tight leg: heating oil is +17.30% on the month and gasoline +6.08%, and US pump diesel has been at record levels, while crude itself has gone flat with WTI at 102.173 and its WEEKLY figure NEGATIVE at -0.30% against +8.95% at this point last week, and Brent at 105.755 and -1.74%. That divergence is the single most useful thing on this page for positioning, because it tells you which headlines move the board: a refinery, products or shipping-insurance story reprices inflation and therefore rates; a crude-flow story increasingly does not. The second live thread is the Saudi East-West pipeline. Drones launched from Iraqi territory struck Petroline on September 10 and 11 while it was carrying roughly 5 million bpd of crude rerouted around Hormuz, putting about 4 million bpd at risk; Wright has said the interruption will be measured in days, prediction markets price an 84.5% chance of a restart by September 30, and Aramco demonstrated after the April strike that it can restore all 7 million bpd within days. Oil has stopped rising into that, which is the market pricing the restart before it happens. The FX read follows directly and produced today's second board change. Canada was cut from +1 to 0 because the terms-of-trade leg that carried it went flat BEFORE its known negative catalyst landed, which is the worst sequencing a commodity-currency trade can have; a currency whose only argument is energy, in a week when energy stopped paying, is a neutral. In the opposite direction, Europe finally caught a break: EU gas is -5.20% on the week, UK gas -6.08% and German gas -2.48% on the day, the first sustained fall since the strikes, which is why EUR holds +1 through a soft wage print and why GER40 came off SHORT. The franc keeps its haven floor - the SNB named Middle East tensions in June as a risk that could STRENGTHEN the franc alongside a signalled readiness to intervene - which is why CHF is held at zero rather than cut despite the widest policy gap on the board. And Japan now carries the energy shock in its external accounts directly, with the trade balance widening to -1,106bn yen from -638bn, a structural drag that tomorrow's 25bp hike does not offset. The standing risk to every one of these positions is the same: this is an active war, the restart is a headline rather than a fact, and a second strike on Petroline or a products-infrastructure hit reverses the oil leg, the gas relief, the Canadian cut and the euro's reprieve in a single session.",

